{"content_hash": "89d4c7c77d4641af", "version_id": 584, "article_id": 59, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Iran war pushes Healey's Budget into tough choices", "title_zh": "伊朗战争让希利的预算面临艰难选择", "has_translation": true, "topic": "business", "source": "bbc_business", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm78pwdp3n5qo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1693139062697-f5f8663217fa?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8MXx8aXJhbiUyMHdhciUyMHRocmVhdGVuc3xlbnwwfDB8fHwxNzkxNzY1NjgyfDA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by sina drakhshani on Unsplash", "author": "sina drakhshani", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@sina_drakhshani?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/flag-of-iran-waving-9S96Rqy9Q1U?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(107, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(145, 46%, 48%)", "label": "BUSINESS", "seed": "56335abab054"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-12", "word_count": 198, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "A leading think tank warns that higher inflation caused by the US-Israel war in Iran is making the Chancellor's first Budget much harder to plan.", "lead_zh": "一家知名智库警告说，美以对伊朗的战争推高了通胀，让财相的第一份预算难做得多。", "preview": "The Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) said rising prices could reduce the money the government hoped to spend on public services. Chancello", "preview_zh": "财政研究所（IFS）表示，物价上涨可能减少政府原本希望用于公共服务的支出。财相约翰·希利现在可能不得不在削减开支、增税或两者兼有之间做出选择。财政部拒绝就可能引发猜测的传言置评。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 572, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/59/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 80.1, "size_bytes": 484416, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 238, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@4c59139/content/data/versions/584.json", "paragraphs": ["The Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) said rising prices could reduce the money the government hoped to spend on public services. Chancellor John Healey may now have to choose between cutting spending, raising taxes, or a mixture of both. The Treasury declined to comment on what it called \"rumour and speculation\".", "Healey said the effects of conflicts in the Middle East and Europe are hitting countries worldwide, leading to higher fuel prices and more expensive mortgages. He promised his Budget would deliver \"fiscal discipline\" and give people \"breathing space\". However, the government is under pressure to show financial markets it controls borrowing, as debt costs rise.", "The IFS cautioned Healey against announcing spending in his 28 October Budget that he has no intention of honouring. Director Helen Miller said a \"steady-as-she-goes\" Budget is not an option, and pencilling in cuts he will not deliver would make it \"a work of fiction\".", "The IFS expects the government to meet its fiscal rules, but with a smaller buffer of around £20bn, less than the £24bn predicted earlier. It warns that forecasts are unusually uncertain, and that keeping departments' budgets in line with inflation may require an extra £25bn by 2029-30."], "paragraphs_zh": ["财政研究所（IFS）表示，物价上涨可能减少政府原本希望用于公共服务的支出。财相约翰·希利现在可能不得不在削减开支、增税或两者兼有之间做出选择。财政部拒绝就可能引发猜测的传言置评。", "希利表示，中东和欧洲冲突的影响正在冲击世界各国，导致燃油价格上涨、国内房贷更贵。他承诺预算将带来“财政纪律”，并给人们“喘息空间”。然而，随着借贷成本上升，政府面临向金融市场证明其能控制借贷的压力。", "财政研究所提醒希利，不要在10月28日的预算演讲中宣布他无意兑现的支出计划。所长海伦·米勒表示，“稳步前行”的预算不是一个选项，而虚列他根本不会实施的削减会让预算变成“虚构作品”。", "财政研究所预计政府将满足其财政规则，但缓冲空间会缩小到约200亿英镑，低于此前预测的240亿英镑。它警告说，预测异常不确定，而要让各部门预算跟上通胀，到2029-30年度可能需要额外250亿英镑。"], "body": "The Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) said rising prices could reduce the money the government hoped to spend on public services. Chancellor John Healey may now have to choose between cutting spending, raising taxes, or a mixture of both. The Treasury declined to comment on what it called \"rumour and speculation\".\n\nHealey said the effects of conflicts in the Middle East and Europe are hitting countries worldwide, leading to higher fuel prices and more expensive mortgages. He promised his Budget would deliver \"fiscal discipline\" and give people \"breathing space\". However, the government is under pressure to show financial markets it controls borrowing, as debt costs rise.\n\nThe IFS cautioned Healey against announcing spending in his 28 October Budget that he has no intention of honouring. Director Helen Miller said a \"steady-as-she-goes\" Budget is not an option, and pencilling in cuts he will not deliver would make it \"a work of fiction\".\n\nThe IFS expects the government to meet its fiscal rules, but with a smaller buffer of around £20bn, less than the £24bn predicted earlier. It warns that forecasts are unusually uncertain, and that keeping departments' budgets in line with inflation may require an extra £25bn by 2029-30.", "body_zh": "财政研究所（IFS）表示，物价上涨可能减少政府原本希望用于公共服务的支出。财相约翰·希利现在可能不得不在削减开支、增税或两者兼有之间做出选择。财政部拒绝就可能引发猜测的传言置评。\n\n希利表示，中东和欧洲冲突的影响正在冲击世界各国，导致燃油价格上涨、国内房贷更贵。他承诺预算将带来“财政纪律”，并给人们“喘息空间”。然而，随着借贷成本上升，政府面临向金融市场证明其能控制借贷的压力。\n\n财政研究所提醒希利，不要在10月28日的预算演讲中宣布他无意兑现的支出计划。所长海伦·米勒表示，“稳步前行”的预算不是一个选项，而虚列他根本不会实施的削减会让预算变成“虚构作品”。\n\n财政研究所预计政府将满足其财政规则，但缓冲空间会缩小到约200亿英镑，低于此前预测的240亿英镑。它警告说，预测异常不确定，而要让各部门预算跟上通胀，到2029-30年度可能需要额外250亿英镑。", "text": "The Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) said rising prices could reduce the money the government hoped to spend on public services. Chancellor John Healey may now have to choose between cutting spending, raising taxes, or a mixture of both. The Treasury declined to comment on what it called \"rumour and speculation\". Healey said the effects of conflicts in the Middle East and Europe are hitting countries worldwide, leading to higher fuel prices and more expensive mortgages. He promised his Budget would deliver \"fiscal discipline\" and give people \"breathing space\". However, the government is under pressure to show financial markets it controls borrowing, as debt costs rise. The IFS cautioned Healey against announcing spending in his 28 October Budget that he has no intention of honouring. Director Helen Miller said a \"steady-as-she-goes\" Budget is not an option, and pencilling in cuts he will not deliver would make it \"a work of fiction\". The IFS expects the government to meet its fiscal rules, but with a smaller buffer of around £20bn, less than the £24bn predicted earlier. It warns that forecasts are unusually uncertain, and that keeping departments' budgets in line with inflation may require an extra £25bn by 2029-30.", "vocab": [{"word": "inflation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "通货膨胀", "note": "经济新闻高频词，指物价普遍上涨", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "think tank", "pos": "noun", "zh": "智库", "note": "研究政策并提供建议的机构", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "Chancellor", "pos": "noun", "zh": "（英国）财政大臣", "note": "英国政府中负责财政的官员", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "fiscal", "pos": "adj", "zh": "财政的", "note": "常与 discipline、rules 搭配", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "credible", "pos": "adj", "zh": "可信的", "note": "形容计划或说法令人信服", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "borrowing", "pos": "noun", "zh": "借贷；借款", "note": "政府借钱需支付利息", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "buffer", "pos": "noun", "zh": "缓冲；缓冲空间", "note": "这里指财政上的余量", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "headroom", "pos": "noun", "zh": "余量；回旋余地", "note": "财经语境中常指财政缓冲", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "pencil in", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "暂定；草拟", "note": "指暂时安排，日后可能更改", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "honour", "pos": "verb", "zh": "兑现；履行", "note": "常与 promise、plan 搭配", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "volatile", "pos": "adj", "zh": "不稳定的；易变的", "note": "常形容市场或局势", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "breathing space", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "喘息空间", "note": "指暂时的缓解或放松", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Iran war threatens Healey's Budget plans, think tank warns", "summary_original": "The impact of the Iran war poses significant risks for the government’s spending plans, the Institute for Fiscal Studies says.", "body_original": "Iran war threatens Healey's Budget plans, think tank warns\nBy\nDharshini David\n,\nDeputy economics editor\nand\nTom Espiner\n,\nBusiness reporter\nPublished\n1 hour ago\nThe price spike caused by the US-Israel war in Iran poses a major challenge for Chancellor John Healey as he puts together his first Budget, an influential think tank has warned.\nHigher-than-expected inflation could eat into what the government hoped to spend on public services, meaning Healey will have to choose between spending cuts, tax rises, or a mixture of both, the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) said.\nIt urged the government to ensure its spending plans are credible, as opposed to proposing unrealistic future cuts to make the Budget add up.\nThe Treasury declined to comment on \"rumour and speculation\".\nHowever, speaking ahead of the annual International Monetary Fund meeting this week, Healey said: \"The consequences of conflict in the Middle East and Europe are hitting countries worldwide, leading to higher prices at the pumps and more expensive mortgages at home.\n\"But Britain and our allies can face up to these challenges together.\"\nHe said his Budget \"will build on this approach with fiscal discipline helping to deliver economic resilience\" and give people \"breathing space\".\nHowever, the government is under growing pressure to show financial markets that it has a tight grip on finances as the cost of borrowing rises.\nThe IFS cautioned Healey about making any spending announcements in the 28 October Budget speech he has no intention of honouring.\n\"A 'steady-as-she-goes' Budget is not an option available to the chancellor,\" said IFS director Helen Miller said.\n\"Choosing to stay the course with the inherited tax and spending plans would mean allowing inflation to blow departments' spending plans off course.\n\"Simply pencilling in implicit cuts that he has no intention of delivering would make the Budget a work of fiction,\" she added.\nThe US-Israel war against Iran and Russia's invasion of Ukraine have together helped drive up energy prices around the world.\nThese higher costs have been passed to customers via higher prices on shelves and at the pumps.\nThe wars have also contributed to a rise in the cost of UK government debt interest payments, adding to the pressure on Healey.\nAlthough higher inflation will boost tax revenues, it will also eat into the value of departments' cash budgets, the IFS said.\n'Breathing space'\nLike other analysts, the IFS thinks recent turbulence on bond markets and higher inflation will add around £10bn to forecasts for the government's borrowing costs in 2029-30.\nHowever, it still expects the government to meet its self-imposed fiscal rules - that it will only borrow for investment projects by the financial year 2029-30 and also be reducing debt as a percentage of GDP - not least as inflation pushes up tax revenues.\nBut it expects those rules to be met by a smaller buffer - known as \"headroom\" - of around £20bn. That is less than the £24bn buffer predicted at the time of the Spring Statement by the official forecaster, the Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR).\nRecent indications have been that the markets would be comfortable with that degree of reduced headroom. The IFS also expects any \"breathing space\" help for households to be temporary and limited in scale.\nHowever, the IFS warns that in such a volatile environment, its forecasts are exceptionally uncertain. And the official forecast from the OBR - which the chancellor relies on when making his plans - may come to a very different conclusion.\nThe IFS reckons that to maintain departments' budgets in the face of higher inflation, the government may need to find an extra £25bn in 2029-30.\nThat is before consider considering the ambition to raise defence spending. Raising such an amount would point to significant tax rises.\nThe chancellor does not have to address spending totals in this Budget, and may choose to leave such decisions until future events - for example, next year's Spring Statement or Spending Review - by which time the outlook for the economy could look very different.\nBut the IFS warned that the spending plans inherited from the previous chancellor, Rachel Reeves, would mean some departments' budgets would not keep pace with inflation in a few years time.\nIt said not addressing this would cast doubt on the credibility of the government's plans.\nHowever, previous chancellors have relied on a similar strategy of pencilling in unrealistic future spending plans to ensure that they meet the fiscal rules on the day - only to change the plans further down the line.\nRelated topics\nUK economy\nInstitute for Fiscal Studies\nJohn Healey\nMore on this story\nWhat is the Budget and what could be in it?\nPublished\n3 days ago", "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T00:41:21.254686", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.287, "sm": 100, "em": 287, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Institute", "s": 0.3, "e": 0.9, "sm": 300, "em": 899, "cs": 4, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "for", "s": 0.912, "e": 1.062, "sm": 912, "em": 1062, "cs": 14, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "Fiscal", "s": 1.075, "e": 1.438, "sm": 1075, "em": 1437, "cs": 18, "ce": 24, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "Studies", "s": 1.45, "e": 1.962, "sm": 1450, "em": 1962, "cs": 25, "ce": 32, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "(", "s": 1.962, "e": 2.138, "sm": 1962, "em": 2137, "cs": 33, "ce": 34, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "IFS", "s": 2.138, "e": 2.775, "sm": 2137, "em": 2775, "cs": 34, "ce": 37, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": ")", "s": 2.775, "e": 3.062, "sm": 2775, "em": 3062, "cs": 37, "ce": 38, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "said", "s": 3.062, "e": 3.263, "sm": 3062, "em": 3262, "cs": 39, "ce": 43, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "rising", "s": 3.275, "e": 3.688, "sm": 3275, "em": 3